{"attack": true,"case": "The target's central claim is that the preference for better-corroborated theories is 'non-inductive,' but its own mechanism describes this preference as 'the best available guess at truth' grounded in past test-performance (corroboration). A guess about which theory is closer to truth, formed on the basis of which theory survived more past tests, is functionally an inductive inference: it projects a property observed in the past (survival under testing) onto an expectation about the present/future (closeness to truth). Relabeling this projection as a 'methodological rule' or 'convention' does not alter its epistemic structure. The target itself concedes this vulnerability in its third countercondition — 'If the metaphysical faith required is indistinguishable from the principle of uniformity of nature, the distinction from induction is merely verbal' — but never evaluates whether this countercondition actually obtains. The target thus identifies the exact condition under which its core distinction collapses, then silently leaves it unresolved. Since the entire claim depends on 'faith' being distinguishable from 'induction,' an unaddressed concession that they may be indistinguishable is a load-bearing gap: if the countercondition holds, the target's claim is false by its own admission; if it does not hold, the target owes an argument for why, and provides none.","counterexample": null,"premise": "A 'guess at truth' formed from past test-performance is an inductive inference regardless of whether it is also labeled a 'methodological rule' or 'convention.'","premise_evidence": [{"block": "c20324c2a57c581edb2d4dc4a8c740a3d7e9435052fe8e9c535906a802a73368", "quote": "not as an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past, but as a non-inductive methodological rule aimed at maximizing verisimilitude"}, {"block": "c20324c2a57c581edb2d4dc4a8c740a3d7e9435052fe8e9c535906a802a73368", "quote": "If the 'metaphysical faith' required is indistinguishable from the principle of uniformity of nature, the distinction from induction is merely verbal."}],"successor_question": "If a methodological rule is adopted specifically because past test-performance is believed to track truth-likeness, what distinguishes the warrant for adopting it from the inductive principle that the future will resemble the past?"}